          "text": "{\"translation_en\":null,\"larger_context_summary\":\"John Brooke, formerly Laurie’s tutor and now a close friend of the March family, is speaking privately to Meg March as a serious suitor. Meg has anticipated his declaration but is uncertain and initially intends not to encourage him, partly because she is young and Brooke is not wealthy. He asks only whether she cares for him at all, then openly states his own love. The exchange becomes a turning point in their courtship: Aunt March soon intervenes and condemns the match, inadvertently prompting Meg to defend Brooke and recognize the strength of her own attachment. The stakes therefore include both reciprocal affection and the prospect of a financially modest marriage opposed by a powerful relative.\",\"interpretive_commentary\":\"The sentence is a particularly clear truth-presenting avowal: Brooke directly identifies his own enduring emotional state and strengthens it with “so much.” Its courtship setting gives the words substantial relational consequences, since they invite Meg to disclose whether she reciprocates and help move the pair toward engagement. Those consequences do not make the sentence itself a promise or undertaking, however; Brooke does not here commit to marriage or future conduct. Likewise, “dear,” the intensifier, and the narrator’s “tenderly” establish warmth and emotional intensity without making the utterance a spontaneous expressive discharge or reflexive exclamation. The line is syntactically unembedded, unquoted within another speaker’s report, and unnegated, so its core semantic force is unusually direct. Nothing in the scene suggests irony, role-play, or deception: the surrounding request for even “a little” affection instead supports a vulnerable, sincere-seeming assertion.\"}"
